From 763dbf41fc6ed2e5c0a10d86e115029798e73ba5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arbion Halili <a.halili@protonmail.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 17:11:42 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] feat(castore/fs): build a metadata index from a set of
 roots
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Add the offline half of the shared index: a walk that resolves the whole
closure of a set of roots through the directory service — every directory
fetched, every `nix-support/fsmeta` declaration evaluated — and turns it
into the flat format.

Inode numbers are assigned here, once, densely and deterministically. The
walk keys each node on exactly what the inode tracker keys on — a file's
(digest, executable, declared-subtree) identity, a directory's (digest,
declared-subtree) identity, a symlink's target — so the index and a live
tracker agree on which nodes share an inode, including the attribute-aware
splits the fsmeta series introduced. Roots are visited in basename order
and each directory's children in stored (sorted) order, first-encounter
assignment counting up from a fixed base, so the walk and the bytes it
produces are a deterministic function of the store's contents.

`build_from_root_nodes` wraps the walk with the declaration-reading the
serving filesystem does, so a caller only supplies the roots and the
convention; a missing or malformed declaration yields the empty one, just
as when serving. Building resolves every directory exactly once, which is
what frees a serving daemon from ever fetching one.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
